course: dictionary
lesson: 9068
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many short words have more than one meaning, and slip is one of them. The words around it tell you which meaning to use. This is context again. «She slipped out of the room» means she left quietly and quickly, but «a slip of the tongue» means a small mistake made by saying the wrong word.
explain: Look at the other words first. Next to wet, ice or a floor, slip means to slide by accident and nearly fall. Next to permission, slip means a small piece of paper. Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it fits.
explain: Some phrases have their own meaning. To slip up means to make a mistake, and to let it slip means to tell a secret by accident. Learn these as whole chunks, not as single words.
fact: Slip is a verb (slip, slips, slipped, slipping) and a noun (a slip, slips).
fact: As a verb, slip can mean to slide by accident on something wet or smooth, or to move quietly and quickly.
fact: As a noun, a slip can be a small mistake, as in «a slip of the tongue», or a small piece of paper, such as a permission slip for a school trip.
fact: Wet floors, ice and spills make people slip, so walk, don't run, and wear shoes with grip.
fact: In Australia, «Slip, slop, slap» means slip on a shirt, slop on sunscreen and slap on a hat.
exercise: «Be careful, the floor is wet and people can slip on it.» What does slip mean here? | *To slide by accident and nearly fall | To go quietly out of a room | To put a note into your pocket quickly | To make a small mistake
exercise: «Reading your work aloud helps you hear tense slips.» What is a tense slip? | *A small mistake in the verb tense | A type of permission slip | A quiet walk out of a room
exercise: Mum says, «You need a permission slip for the trip.» What is a permission slip? | A slide on the ice | A quick move out of the door | *A small piece of paper
test: «She let it slip that the party was on Friday.» What does let it slip mean? | To paint a picture slowly | *To tell a secret by accident | To go to sleep early | To hide a present under the bed
test: «Slip on a shirt, slop on sunscreen, slap on a hat.» Which country uses this advice? | Canada | *Australia | Scotland | Germany
exam: Which sentence uses slip as a noun? | «She slipped out of the room.» | «She slipped the note into her bag.» | *«He made a slip of the tongue at dinner.» | «Mind you don't slip on the ice.»
